    <meta name="dc.description" content="This study investigates the formation and characterization of self&#8211;organized TiO2 coatings on titanium foil via anodization to enhance surface&#8211;dependent mechanical and electrochemical properties for biomedical applications. Anodization was performed in an ethylene glycol-based organic electrolyte containing 0.4 wt.% NH4F at ambient temperature, with applied voltages ranging from 15 to 60&amp;nbsp;V and durations from 0.5 to 9&amp;nbsp;h. The resulting nanostructured TiO2 coatings exhibited morphology and dimensions dependent on process parameters. TiO2-coated surfaces showed significantly reduced nano&#8211;hardness (2.43 GPa for anodized at 45&amp;nbsp;V) compared to the untreated Ti foil (3.4&amp;nbsp;GPa). The TiO2 nanofilm exhibited hydrophilic behaviour, with a contact angle of 0&#176; at 60&amp;nbsp;V. Corrosion resistance and bioactivity were notably improved following anodization. These findings suggest that optimizing anodization parameters can effectively tailor surface properties, making them suitable for biomedical implant applications and corrosion&#8211;resistant coatings."/>
